# Line endings, normalised in the repository.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true` and the repository had no `.gitattributes`,
# so ten files carried CRLF in the index and printed
#
#     warning: in the working copy of '<path>', LF will be replaced by CRLF the
#     next time Git touches it
#
# on every `git diff` any session ran. The warning is noise, but it is noise on the
# one command three sessions use to see each other's work, and all three independently
# named this file as the fix.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes LF the stored form and the checked-out form, which is the
# end state all three sessions named.
#
# MEASURED, so nobody expects more of this file than it delivers: adding it does NOT
# silence the warning today. It inverts it. Before: 8 warnings, all "LF will be
# replaced by CRLF" (files whose working copy is LF). After: 7 warnings, all "CRLF
# will be replaced by LF" — a disjoint set, being the files whose working copy is
# CRLF from having been checked out under `autocrlf` before this file existed. Each
# one clears the first time that file is staged, and the set empties completely only
# after the renormalisation pass below. The `git diff` output itself is unaffected:
# no file shows a spurious whole-file rewrite, which was the risk worth checking.
#
# THE RENORMALISATION PASS IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE. `git add --renormalize .`
# rewrites line endings across the whole tree, which would rewrite files other
# sessions are actively editing — there were 13 dirty files in another session's
# working set when this landed. Adding the file alone lets normalisation take effect
# per-file as each is next touched, which is the incremental, conflict-free path. The
# sweep is a separate, operator-timed step.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never attempts a line-ending translation or a textual diff on them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip binary
*.pyc binary
